Electrochemical water splitting provides an attractive way to produce hydrogen fuel. Unfortunately, the efficient and largescale $\Hat{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ production is still hindered by the sluggish kinetics of oxygen evolution reaction (OER) at the anode side of a water electrolyzer. Starting from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), we demonstrate a template-engaged strategy to transform Ni-Ni Prussian blue analogue (PBA) nanoplates into porous carbon coated nickel phosphides nanoplates with mixed phases of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{5}{\mathsf{P}}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ . For comparison, NiO and $N i(O H)_{2}$ porous nanoplates with the similar morphology have also been synthesized from the same precursor. Benefitting from their structural merits and insitu formed catalytically active oxidized nickel species, the asderived nickel phosphides manifest excellent electrocatalytic activity for OER superior to NiO and $N i(O H)_{2}$ .
Electrochemical splitting of water into hydrogen and oxygen is considered to be one of the easiest and cleanest methods to obtain hydrogen.1, 2 However, it still requires high overpotentials for both the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) to obtain decent reaction rates.2-4 The OER has been regarded as a rate-limiting step in realizing a fully integrated water-splitting system because of the high energy barrier for $0\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bond breaking and attendant $_{0^{-}0}$ bond formation.5-7 Therefore, an efficient electrocatalyst is highly desired to address this challenge by effectively coupling multiple proton and electron transfers for $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ evolution at low overpotentials (η). Currently, ruthenium (Ru) and iridium (Ir) oxides have been regarded as the most efficient OER electrocatalysts.7 However, their scarcity and high price greatly limit their practical applications. Tremendous efforts have been made to searching for highly active, stable, and low cost OER electrocatalysts.7-11 Among them, nickel-based hydroxides and oxides have shown outstanding performance for OER.5, 10, 12-20 For example, Gao et al. reported that $a-N i(O H)_{2}$ hollow spheres are remarkably active and stable OER catalyst in alkaline media.5 Nai et al. fabricated amorphous Ni-Co double hydroxides nanocages with optimized Ni/Co ratio for efficient OER.15
Metal (including cobalt, nickel, and iron) phosphides have been extensively investigated as high-performance electrocatalysts for HER.21-37 However, the investigation into the application of metal phosphides as OER electrocatalysts is still rarely reported.38, 39 Most recently, Hu et al. for the first time showed that ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ is also highly active for oxygen evolution.38 They found that ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ electrocatalysts exhibit better OER performance than nickel oxides and nickel hydroxides.38 However, when comparing the electrocatalytic activities among different nickel-based electrocatalysts, the catalysts in most works are usually synthesized using different precursors or templates and with different morphologies.21, 38 To get insights into the activity difference among various catalysts, it is desirable to synthesize several kinds of catalysts with similar morphologies from one single precursor or template. Herein, Ni-based metal organic-framework (MOF) $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}]$ Prussian blue analogue (PBA) nanoplates) is used as the precursor to obtain nickel phosphides, nickel oxides, and nickel hydroxides with similar porous plate-like nanostructures. Remarkably, nickel phosphides (denoted  as Ni-P) porous nanoplates with mixed phases of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{5}{\mathsf{P}}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ not only exhibit better OER activity in comparison to nickel oxides and nickel hydroxides porous nanoplates and many non-noble metal-based catalysts reported to date but also efficiently catalyze the HER in acid solution. Its outstanding electrocatalytic activity can be attributed to the porous nanostructure, the incorporation of amorphous carbon from MOF, and the in-situ generated oxidized nickel species serving as active sites for OER.
The strategy to synthesize ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2},$ NiO, and Ni-P porous nanoplates is schematically shown in Fig. 1. $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}]$ PBA (NiNi-PBA) nanoplates are first synthesized through a facile coprecipitation method40 and used as the templates. $N i(O H)_{2}$ porous nanoplates are obtained by reaction between the NiNi-PBA precursors and NaOH solution $(2.0~\mathsf{M})$ , which is driven by the high concentration of hydroxide ions. In the alkaline solution, an ion exchange reaction takes place as described below: $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}](\mathsf{s})+2\mathsf{O H}^{-}(\mathsf{a q.})\to\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}(\mathsf{s})$ $+\ N i(C N)_{4}^{2-}(\mathsf{a q.})$ . The as-synthesized NiNi-PBA nanoplates are calcinated in air atmosphere to prepare nanoporous NiO nanoplates. Ni-P porous nanoplates are fabricated through a low-temperature phosphidation of NiNi-PBA templates under argon atmosphere.
The formation of NiNi-PBA is confirmed by X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis, as shown in Fig. 2a. All of the diffraction peaks could be unambiguously assigned to the Hofmann-type ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}})_{2}[{\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{C N}})_{4}]\cdot\times{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ with a 2D layered structure, which is consistent with the reported work.40 No additional peaks from impurities are detected, indicating the high purity of the product. Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) image shows that all the products exhibit a 2D plate-like morphology (Fig. 2b). The lateral sizes of the nanoplates are in the range of $300{-}400~\mathsf{n m}$ , while the thicknesses range from 40 to $50\ \mathsf{n m}$ . A magnified FESEM image (Fig. 2c) reveals the smooth surface of the nanoplates, confirming the singlecrystalline nature. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image clearly demonstrates its dense nature without discernible porosity (Fig. 2d).
After low-temperature phosphidation of the NiNi-PBA nanoplates, the product is composed of a mixture of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{5}{\mathsf{P}}_{4}$ (JCPDS no. 18-883) and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ (JCPDS no. 65-1989) (Fig. S1, ESI), with ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ as the major component. Following phosphidation, the FESEM image shows that the 2D morphology is well maintained while the surface of the nanoplates becomes rough (Fig. 3a). A high-resolution FESEM image reveals the highly porous nature of Ni-P nanoplates (Fig. 3b). Fig. 3c and d show the FESEM and TEM images of one single Ni-P nanoplate, respectively, confirming its rough and porous feature. The energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) elemental mapping images (Fig. 3e and f) further demonstrate that both Ni and P elements are uniformly distributed throughout the whole nanoplate. The corresponding EDX spectrum shows the atomic ratio between
Ni and P to be 21:17 and the existence of higVhiewcoArntitclenOtnlinoef carbon $(30.9\ \mathrm{wt}.\%)$ in the nanoplate (FiDg.OI:S120, 1E0S3I9)/.CT6EhEe00h1i0g0hAresolution TEM image further verifies that nickel phosphides are covered by amorphous carbon layer with a thickness of around $2-3~\mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. S3, ESI). The as-synthesized carbon coated nickel phosphides porous nanoplates exhibit a specific surface area of $35{\mathsf{m}}^{2}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ (Fig. S4, ESI).
The as-synthesized NiNi-PBA nanoplates are transformed into NiO porous nanoplates by annealing in air. All the diffraction peaks of the calcinated products can be indexed to the cubic phase of NiO (JCPDS no. 04-0835) (Fig. S5a, ESI). No other peaks from impurity are observed, indicating a complete thermal conversion of the NiNi-PBA precursors into NiO. The plate-like morphology is well preserved after calcination (Fig. S5b and c, ESI). TEM observation reveals the highly porous structure of NiO nanoplates (Fig. S5d, ESI). The ion exchange reaction between NiNi-PBA and NaOH is verified by Fourier transform-infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR). The peaks of C-N stretching located at $2170\ c m^{-1}$ disappear after the reaction, indicating the removal of ${\mathsf{N i}}{(\mathsf{C N})_{4}}^{2-}$ from NiNi-PBA precursors (Fig. S6a, ESI). The products after ion exchange are nearly amorphous as determined by XRD analysis (Fig. S6b, ESI). The original bright blue color of NiNi-PBA precursors turns to green after reacting with NaOH (inset in Fig. S6b, ESI). XPS analysis is performed to confirm the successful synthesis of $N i(O H)_{2}$ . The typical Ni $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ and $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ peaks of as-prepared products with two shake-up satellites are obtained at 873.4 and $855.6\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Fig. S6c, ESI). The spin-energy separation of 17.8 eV is the characteristic of the $N i(O H)_{2}$ phase, which is in good agreement with the reported results.41, 42 The O 1s highresolution spectrum (Fig. S6d, ESI) shows three oxygen contributions. The peak located at $529.9\mathrm{~eV}$ is typical of metaloxygen bonds. The peak at $531.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ is associated with oxygen in $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ groups. And the peak at $532.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to physic- and chemi-absorbed molecular water. The as-prepared
$N i(O H)_{2}$ product retains the plate-like morphology with porous structure after ion exchange (Fig. S6e, f, ESI).
The OER activity of the as-synthesized Ni-P, NiO, and $N i(O H)_{2}$ nanoplates is characterized in a three-electrode electrochemical cell with a rotating disk electrode (RDE) configuration. The polarization curves (Fig. 4) show that Ni-P nanoplates exhibit higher current density and a more negative OER onset potential (1.48 V vs. RHE) than $N i(O H)_{2}$ nanoplates (1.49 V vs. RHE) and NiO nanoplates (1.58 V vs. RHE). We further compare the operating potential required for different electrocatalysts to deliver a current density of $10\ m A\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which is a metric related to solar fuel synthesis.8 The Ni-P nanoplates electrode can deliver a current density of $10\mathsf{m}\mathsf{A}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at $\boldsymbol{1.53\mathrm{~v~}}$ (correlated to an overpotential of $300\mathrm{\mV})$ , lower than that of $N i(O H)_{2}$ at $\phantom{-}1.59~\mathsf{V}$ and NiO at $\displaystyle1.66~\mathsf{V}.$ . The oxidation peaks at around 1.37 V vs. RHE in the LSV curves for Ni-P and $N i(O H)_{2}$ are characteristics of the oxidation of Ni(II)/Ni(III) in many nickel oxide/hydroxide-based electrocatalysts.5, 38, 43 This oxidation peak is also reversible for Ni-P from cyclic voltammogram (Fig. S7, ESI). This indicates that nickel oxides/hydroxides are possibly in-situ generated on the surface of Ni-P during the OER process.38 Moreover, this excellent OER activity of Ni-P nanoplates is better than that of most of the state-of-the-art nickel-based electrocatalysts and many other non-noble metal based electrocatalysts in alkaline solution. A detailed comparison of different highly active OER electrocatalysts with various compositions (Table S1, ESI) confirms the outstanding electrocatalytic behavior of Ni-P nanoplates. In addition, the electrocatalytic kinetics for OER is examined by Tafel plots (Fig. 4b). The Tafel slope of Ni-P nanoplates $(64~\mathrm{~mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ is much smaller than those of $N i(O H)_{2}$ nanoplates $\left(111\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}\right)$ ) and NiO nanoplates $(81~\mathsf{m V}$ dec-1) and comparable to those of the previously reported active OER electrocatalysts (Table S1, ESI). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) analysis is performed on Ni-P, ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2},$ and NiO nanoplates at different overpotentials (Fig. S8, ESI). It can be seen that the EIS Nyquist plots of all the three electrocatalysts exhibit two time constants. The first one at high frequency is related to the surface porVioeswi tAryt cloefOntlihne electrode and the second one at low frDeOqI:u1e0.n1c0y3 9r/eCf6lEeEc0ts01t0h0Ae charge transfer process, depending on the overpotential during OER. In general, the charge-transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ shows strong correlation with the electrochemical performance for OER. One can see that for all the samples the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ substantially reduces at high overpotentials. However, when the Nyquist plots of Ni-P, ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2},$ and NiO nanoplates are compared at the same overpotential $(180~\mathsf{m V})$ (Fig. S8d, ESI), Ni-P nanoplates show much lower $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ than ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2},$ and NiO nanoplates, in consistent with their different OER activities. In addition, it should be noted that there are sudden increases in the resistance at high potentials for Ni-P, which may be attributed to the in-situ formed nickel oxide/hydroxides layers.
To assess the durability of the Ni-P electrocatalysts, linear potential sweeps are conducted repeatedly on the electrodes (Fig. S9, ESI). Only little anodic current loss is observed for Ni-P nanoplates after 1000 continuous cycles at an accelerated scanning rate of $20\mathsf{m}\mathsf{v}\ s^{-1}$ . The $\mathbf{\xi}_{I-t}$ measurement further reveals that they can maintain good catalytic activity for at least $10\mathsf{h}$ in alkaline solution (Fig. S10, ESI). This suggests that the electrocatalyst is very stable. Furthermore, the electrocatalytic property of Ni-P nanoplates for HER in acidic solution is also investigated (Fig. S11, ESI). The Ni-P nanoplates electrode shows comparable or even better electrocatalytic property than most of other reported nickel phosphides electrocatalysts for HER in acidic solution (Table S2, ESI). To be specific, to achieve a current density of $10\ m\mathsf{A\ c m^{-2}}$ , the Ni-P nanoplates electrocatalyst only requires overpotential of 110 mV (Fig. S11a, ESI). The Tafel slop of Ni-P nanoplates is $73~\mathsf{m v}$ dec-1 (Fig. S11b, ESI), suggesting a Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism for HER. Also, the superior durability of the electrocatalytic response for HER is confirmed by cycling the Ni-P nanoplates electrode continuously for 1000 cycles (Fig. S11a, ESI).
To get insight into the OER mechanism of Ni-P, the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of Ni-P before and after OER test at $_{1.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ are analyzed and compared (Fig. S12, ESI).  The high-resolution Ni 2p XPS spectrum of the Ni-P after OER test shows an intensity increase at around $856.4\ \mathrm{eV},$ , which can be assigned to the oxidized Ni species44, 45 and a significant intensity decrease at about 852.4 eV (corresponding to $\mathsf{N i}^{\delta+}$ in the Ni-P compound),44, 45 demonstrating the surface oxidation of Ni-P (Fig. S12a, ESI). For the $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ counterpart (Fig. S12b, ESI), the lower binding energy peaks located at around 129.1 eV correspond to P 2p line and another contribution at around 133.6 eV can be assigned to the oxidized phosphate species.46 The relative high intensity of oxidized phosphate species and the nearly disappearance of the phosphide components around binding energy of 129.1 eV further confirm the oxidation of Ni-P.46 The two intense bands in the O 1s spectrum at 531.3 and $530.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ reveal the abundant surface hydroxyls and oxygen from nickel oxides/hydroxides (Fig. S13, ESI).46 In addition, the atomic ratio of O to P on the surface of Ni-P increases from $40:13$ to $43:3$ after the OER test. TEM image shows that a number of nanoparticles with a size of several nanometers are formed on the surface of Ni-P nanoplates after the OER test (Fig. S14a, ESI). The corresponding interplanar spacing is referred to specific facets of nickel oxides/hydroxides (Fig. S15, ESI).38 EDX elemental mapping results demonstrate that the Ni-P after OER test is mainly composed of Ni, P, and C elements, plus a large concentration of O element (Fig. S14a-e, ESI). Clearly, the P element is still dominant in the inner layer, revealing the NiP/nickel oxide/hydroxide core-shell structure. The above results indicate that the nickel phosphides are partially oxidized to nickel oxides/hydroxides on the surface during the OER process consistent with other reports.38, 39, 47 The in-situ generated oxidized nickel species then serve as primary active sites for OER. Such an oxidation process has been reported by other metal phosphides-based electrocatalysts for OER as well.38, 39, 46-48
The outstanding OER electrocatalytic activities of the Ni-P nanoplates can be mainly attributed to the following aspects. First, the incorporation of amorphous carbon into nickel phosphides, achieved by the MOF-assisted synthesis method, improves the conductivity and charge transfer capability. Second, the uniform morphology and porous structure are expected to maximize the number of exposed active sites for electrochemical reactions, and to facilitate theViedwiffArutiscileoOnnlionef reactants in the electrolyte. Third, the inD-sOiIt:u10f.1o0r3m9/eCd6EoEx0i0d1iz0e0Ad nickel species on the surface of Ni-P can act as active sites for catalytic reactions, and on the other hand the metallic nickel phosphides with high electrical conductivity can facilitate electron transfer.38, 46-48 In addition, the nickel phosphates may also enhance the OER kinetics by accelerating proton transfer during water oxidation catalysis.46-48 The synergistic effects from these aspects contribute to the improved OER performance of carbon coated nickel phosphides porous nanoplates.
## Conclusions
In summary, we have developed a simple MOF-assisted strategy to synthesize Ni-P, ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2},$ and NiO porous nanoplates as electrocatalysts for OER from Ni-Ni Prussian blue analogue precursors. Benefiting from their desirable nanostructure and in-situ generated OER active oxidized nickel species, the Ni-P porous nanoplates show much enhanced electrocatalytic activity for OER than $N i(O H)_{2}$ and NiO porous nanoplates. An overpotential as low as $300~\mathrm{{mV}}$ is required to attain a current density of $10\ m\mathsf{A}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which is favorably comparable to those of recently reported non-noble metalbased OER electrocatalysts. In addition, the Ni-P porous nanoplates exhibit excellent electrocatalytic activity for HER in acidic solution. Furthermore, they also show reasonably good long-term stability in both alkaline and acidic solutions. These findings not only extend the family of MOF-derived functional nanomaterials but also highlight the potential application of nickel phosphides as efficient electrocatalysts for OER.
